From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:22:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Deliver a connection's results in request order on
 one channel

Every request had its own result callback, one IPC channel and router
route each in multiprocess mode, and script fired a request's events
when its result arrived, so results of one transaction could overtake
each other. Commit results came on yet another callback per commit,
and auto-commit needed two extra round trips: script told the backend
each request it had handled (RequestHandled), the backend asked script
to recheck the commit (TxnMaybeCommit), and only then script sent
Commit. An abort waited for the backend's reply before firing anything,
results that arrived after it still fired success events, and the
pending requests never got their AbortError error events.

Now script creates one result channel per open request, which becomes
the connection's (design decision 9). The backend keeps it with the
open request, the connection and each of its transactions. The open
request's messages go on it (ConnectionEvent::Open, which replaces the
factory's callback), so the success that creates the IDBDatabase comes
before any event of the connection; on two channels, a versionchange
could overtake it under --multiprocess and be dropped. So does
everything about the connection's transactions, tagged with the
transaction's serial number: each request's result with its request
id (TransactionEvent::Request, whose RequestOutput replaces the typed
callbacks), a failed schema operation (SchemaChangeFailed), and the
commit result (Committed). The versionchange event of the connection
uses it too, instead of the factory's channel. The actor sends them in
the order it runs the work, and the channel keeps that order, so a
large getAll can't be overtaken by the small results after it. The
operations lost their callbacks, and the manager fails a request for
a database or transaction it doesn't know with DbNotFound on the
connection's channel, as it does a Commit. Script keeps no sender: once the connection is
closed and its transactions have finished, the backend drops the last
one, which removes the route and releases the Trusted open request
that the channel's callback holds.

IDBTransaction keeps the spec's request list: the requests whose
result task hasn't run, in request order, each with its result once it
has arrived. Results are delivered from the front only (a result that
arrives before an earlier one waits), each in its own task, through
IDBRequest::convert_result and finish_with_success/finish_with_error,
which run "fire a success/error event" as before. IDBDatabase routes
the connection's events to its live transactions by serial.

Auto-commit is decided in script: a transaction that is inactive, or
committing after commit(), sends Commit once its request list is
empty. The manager commits once the transaction can start and all its
requests have been handed to the engine, which runs the commit after
them; RequestHandled, TxnMaybeCommit (both directions), TxnCompleteMsg
and the handled-request bookkeeping on both sides are gone. commit()
sends Commit when the results of the requests made before it have been
delivered, so that a request error while committing can still abort
the transaction (asynchronously execute a request, step 5.3): the
erroring request stays in the list and gets the abort's AbortError.

The manager registers a transaction only for a connection it has open,
so that every registered transaction has a channel; one created for a
connection the backend dropped would hold back every later writer and
the readers of its scope, with nowhere to send its results. Script
creates none: an aborted upgrade transaction closes its connection
(open a database connection, step 10.8), so transaction() throws
InvalidStateError from the abort event on. The manager also checks each
request against its transaction, as script does before sending it: a
schema operation needs an upgrade transaction, a write a readwrite or
upgrade one, and the store must be in the scope. A request that breaks
these rules fails and aborts the transaction, and the requests and the
commit of an aborted transaction fail.

"Abort a transaction" runs in script at once: Abort goes to the
backend (no reply; the actor rolls back after the requests it was
handed, before anything sent later), an upgrade transaction reverts
the connection's version and metadata, every request still in the
request list gets a queued error event with AbortError, and a queued
task then fires abort, which now bubbles as the spec says, and sends
TransactionFinished. Results arriving after the abort find no request
and are discarded. abort() on an aborted transaction throws
InvalidStateError. The teardown abort of P1 also empties the request
list.

The open request's error dispatch moves from IDBFactory to
IDBOpenDBRequest, and with its two message-less Error::Version and
Error::Abort uses, two allowances in tidy's
python/tidy/error_message_exceptions.txt move along.

Tests: the backend and manager tests route request results by id and
collect schema failures per batch; the integration tests open real
connections with a result channel and route its events, and check
that a connection's results arrive in request order across two
transactions with a 4 MiB getAll first, that a request and the commit
of an unknown transaction fail, that the channel stays while a
transaction of a closed connection is live and goes away after it,
that a transaction of a dropped connection holds back no one, that
requests outside their transaction's mode or scope fail, and that a
versionchange follows the open's success on the channel. Fork WPT tests
in tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/: request-result-order.any.js
checks event order after a large getAll in readonly and readwrite
transactions, an abort while results are on their way, commit() with
pending requests, and a cursor continuing after other requests;
connection-after-aborted-upgrade.any.js that the connection of an
aborted upgrade is closed while other connections work; and
versionchange-after-open-success.any.js that versionchange reaches a
connection after its open's success.

Validation: measured at the phase tip after the P2 review fixes folded into this commit. Host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 115 passed, 1 skipped (P2.3 gate: 112). DAYU200 `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage`: RDB only 116 passed, 3 skipped (113); SQLite and RDB 201 passed, 4 skipped (195). `./mach check` here and at each later code commit, and `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` with SQLite, RDB and both: no warning. `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release`, and `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --locked --ohos --profile checked-release` for the default and harmonyos flavors, each with a signed HAP: no warning; `./mach test-unit --profile checked-release`: 1,318 passed, 1 skipped, and with --doc: pass; `./mach test-tidy`: only the known libsqlite3-sys advisory. WPT IndexedDB/ (4,511 subtests): host SpiderMonkey, 8 processes, 3,289 pass (P2.3 gate: 3,289); QuickJS 3,290 (3,290); DAYU200, 2 processes, 3,281 (3,286), as the board is slower now: interleaved-cursors-large times out in its three globals with the baseline binary too. The other unexpected results are order effects: idbfactory-origin-isolation times out after idbcursor-continuePrimaryKey-exception-order.any.worker ran in the same browser, and a worker test whose sharedworker variant, which uses the same database names, ran first in the browser fails, idbcursor-iterating-update.any.worker on host QuickJS and idbversionchangeevent.any.worker on the board; the P2.3 binary does the same in that order, which follows PYTHONHASHSEED. idbindex-rename-abort.any.sharedworker, which timed out on the board, passes alone. /_mozilla/IndexedDB/: 106 of 106 on host SpiderMonkey, also with --multiprocess, on QuickJS and on the board; the P2.3 binary fails connection-after-aborted-upgrade in all its globals, and the P2.1 binary the abort subtest of request-result-order. The two tidy allowances were left behind by the P2 review fold and folded in from a later fix at P3.4, which ran `./mach test-tidy` at this commit again: only the known libsqlite3-sys advisory.
